<h1 class="p-name">United States Protecting Russian Refineries?</h1>
<img class="featured u-featured" src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/54768845290_8ac618dc63.jpg" alt="United States Protecting Russian Refineries?">
<div class="pubinfo"><p>Published by <a class="p-author h-card" href="https://jgregorymcverry.com">Greg McVerry</a> on <time class="dt-published" datetime="2026-10-11">2026-10-11</time> · <a class="u-url" href="https://jgregorymcverry.com/refinery-protection">Permalink</a></p></div>
<div class="e-content copy"><p>On October 10, 2026, President Trump called for Ukraine to replace President Volodymyr Zelensky and again demanded an end to attacks on Russian refineries. The remarks came one day after he announced a deal with Putin to release Russian diesel onto global markets. His criticism of Zelensky tied the political dispute directly to diesel supplies. [34] [36]</p>

<p>People seemed shocked by the confrontation in Washington. But perhaps the President's statements affirmed what had already emerged as an American foreign-policy priority: protecting Russian refinery capacity.</p>

<p>On September 1, 2026, Reuters reported that Russia had cut its oil-production forecast to a 17-year low. The draft projection put annual output at 494.2 million tonnes and petroleum-product exports at 98.5 million tonnes, down from a previous forecast of 122.6 million. Russia entered September with diesel-export restrictions already extended through the end of the month. [1] [2]</p>

<p>As Ukraine's Deep Strike campaign damaged Russian refining and fuel infrastructure, diesel prices climbed amid supply disruptions from both the Iran war and the Russia-Ukraine war. Meanwhile, Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner met Russian officials. Senior American officials repeatedly emphasized Ukraine's attacks when explaining fuel costs that were breaking records. Ukraine's pressure on Russia was increasingly presented as a problem for American consumers. [3] [11] [14] [17] [20] [25]</p>

<p>Media appearances and publications clustered around September 2, September 5-6, September 11-13, and September 24-28. Meetings involving Witkoff and Kushner overlapped with some of these bursts of public messaging. Trump subsequently repeated the refinery argument in Ireland and aboard Air Force One. That sequence raises a question about the relationship between diplomacy and the public explanation of diesel prices; timing alone does not establish that the meetings caused the interviews.</p>

<p>Protecting Russian refineries became a major American foreign-policy priority. </p>

<h2>September 2: the public explanation takes shape</h2>

<p>The day after the Russian forecast report, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent appeared on Fox &amp; Friends. He linked Ukrainian attacks on Russian energy assets and refined products to upward pressure on global prices, while also acknowledging the U.S.-Iran war. His comments followed another reported Ukrainian attack on the Ust-Luga processing and export complex on September 1. [3] [5] [9]</p>

