<h2>September 5-6: diplomacy, Sunday interviews and another refinery strike</h2>

<p>The next burst of media mentions brought diplomacy and fuel messaging into the same weekend. On September 5, Witkoff and Kushner met Vladimir Putin in Moscow. They went to Kyiv on September 6 for talks with Ukrainian officials. Those meetings followed CIA Director John Ratcliffe's late-August Moscow visit, which reporting described as a warning against Russian escalation. [11] [12] [35]</p>

<p>Cabinet members began to squawk about diesel prices on television. In Fox News clips dated September 5, Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy emphasized Russia's importance as a diesel exporter and the pressure created by halted exports. Commentary published September 6 helped circulate the interview. These interviews got clipped and spread through social media. [13]</p>

<p>On September 6, Energy Secretary Chris Wright delivered a sharper version of that explanation on CNN. Asked about record diesel prices, he described Ukrainian destruction of Russian refineries as the single biggest factor. CBS also carried Wright's refinery argument.  The Moscow and Kyiv visits, meanwhile, supplied the diplomatic backdrop. [14] [15]</p>

<p>Simultaneously, Ukraine struck the Ryazan refinery on September 6. The weekend followed September 4 attacks on two fuel depots near Sochi. Thus, the diplomatic visit, Cabinet interviews and attacks on Russian fuel infrastructure occupied the same short window. [10] [16] [37] [38]</p>

