<h2>September 24-28: a meeting, export-policy debate and renewed strikes</h2>

<p>Another burst of media occurred in late September. The discussion had already begun before the September 24 meeting. On September 22, Trump publicly supported considering restrictions on American diesel exports, while Bessent said the administration was examining whether a full or partial ban was feasible. This moved the discussion from blaming supply losses abroad toward deciding what the United States might do at home. [24] Trump may have sought someone to blame for high Diesel prices given his well-documented inability to admit fault. US Special envoys may have also dangled money and energy development deals to entice Russia to the bargaining table.</p>

<p>On September 24, Witkoff and Kushner met Russian envoy Kirill Dmitriev in New York, according to CNN. They U.S.-Russian cooperation and a possible settlement of the war.  September 24 also produced substantial reporting on diesel policy. Bloomberg reported that Trump's advisers were studying a possible short-term export ban, while coverage of Wright's exchanges with industry described disagreement over the consequences. [25] [26] [27]</p>

<p>Ukraine's Deep Strike campaign continued through that debate. Attacks were reported against the Perm and Novoshakhtinsk refineries on September 25 and Ilsky on September 26. On September 28, the SBU named four oil depots in Krasnodar Krai: Angelinskaya, Edelweiss-95, Din-Yug-Oil and Lukoil-Yugnefteprodukt in Pavlovskaya. [28] [29] [31]</p>

<p>September 26 supplied another administration voice: U.N. Ambassador Mike Waltz discussed the economic effects of the Russia-Ukraine war on Fox News. The Washington Examiner framed his appearance as part of the administration's diesel-price messaging. By September 28, coverage was again circulating Trump's refinery argument. The publication date of that later coverage should not be moved into September 24-26 to make the cluster look tighter. [30] [42] [45]</p>

